1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,838
Hello, friend. Come in, come in. Mind the
poppies.

2
00:00:12,888 --> 00:00:17,947
Get yourself comfortable... pull the
blanket up a little higher...

3
00:00:17,997 --> 00:00:21,988
and let your shoulders drop, just a
little.

4
00:00:22,038 --> 00:00:28,164
Welcome to the Cave of Sleep. Far away, at
the very edge of the world, where the

5
00:00:28,214 --> 00:00:33,958
sun never quite reaches, there is a quiet
cave beside a slow, silver river.

6
00:00:34,008 --> 00:00:37,999
Poppies grow at the entrance. No rooster
crows here.

7
00:00:38,049 --> 00:00:43,336
No dog barks. Even the wind takes its
shoes off at the door.

8
00:00:43,386 --> 00:00:49,512
And inside, on a soft dark couch piled with
soft dark blankets, lives the god of

9
00:00:49,562 --> 00:00:52,104
sleep himself. Say hello, Hypnos.

10
00:00:52,554 --> 00:00:53,723
HYPNOS: Hello.

11
00:00:53,773 --> 00:00:57,834
HYPNOS: Please keep your voice down. This
is a cave of sleep.

12
00:00:57,884 --> 00:00:59,662
HYPNOS: It says so on the sign.

13
00:01:00,112 --> 00:01:01,864
There is no sign.

14
00:01:02,314 --> 00:01:04,302
HYPNOS: There's a spiritual sign.

15
00:01:04,752 --> 00:01:08,063
Hypnos is going to help me tell tonight's
story.

16
00:01:08,113 --> 00:01:13,757
He is the son of Night herself, he has
little feathered wings just above his ears,

17
00:01:13,807 --> 00:01:18,285
and he has put every god on Mount Olympus
to sleep at least once.

18
00:01:18,335 --> 00:01:19,383
Including Zeus.

19
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:23,346
HYPNOS: We don't talk about the Zeus
thing. Long story.

20
00:01:23,396 --> 00:01:29,137
HYPNOS: Hera asked for a favor, Zeus woke up
very cranky, and I had to hide behind my

21
00:01:29,187 --> 00:01:31,290
HYPNOS: mother for a while.

22
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:33,889
HYPNOS: Anyway. What are we doing tonight?

23
00:01:34,339 --> 00:01:37,824
Tonight, we are going back to ancient
Greece...

24
00:01:37,874 --> 00:01:41,874
to the very first day in the life of a
very small god.

25
00:01:41,924 --> 00:01:47,692
A god who was born at sunrise. Who
invented a musical instrument by lunchtime.

26
00:01:47,742 --> 00:01:53,437
And who, before the sun went down, stole
fifty cows from his own big brother.

27
00:01:53,887 --> 00:01:55,404
HYPNOS: On his first day.

28
00:01:55,854 --> 00:01:57,371
On his first day.

29
00:01:57,821 --> 00:02:03,162
HYPNOS: I didn't do anything on my first
day. I think I took a nap.

30
00:02:03,212 --> 00:02:06,130
HYPNOS: Honestly, that's still my main
skill.

31
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:09,882
So here's the plan. I'll tell the story.

32
00:02:09,932 --> 00:02:11,598
Hypnos will... help.

33
00:02:12,048 --> 00:02:16,517
HYPNOS: I'll be supervising. From this
couch. With my eyes closed.

34
00:02:16,967 --> 00:02:20,670
And you, my friend, don't have to do
anything at all.

35
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:26,438
You don't have to remember any names. You
don't have to stay awake for the ending.

36
00:02:26,488 --> 00:02:30,747
If you drift off somewhere in the middle,
that's not failing.

37
00:02:30,797 --> 00:02:32,484
That's winning.

38
00:02:32,534 --> 00:02:34,221
Ready?

39
00:02:34,271 --> 00:02:35,125
Let's begin.

40
00:02:48,775 --> 00:02:54,782
Long, long ago, in a part of Greece called
Arcadia, there was a mountain named

41
00:02:54,832 --> 00:03:01,145
Kyllene. It was green and quiet and covered
in pine trees, and on its side, hidden

42
00:03:01,195 --> 00:03:06,129
behind curtains of ivy, there was a deep,
shadowy cave.

43
00:03:06,179 --> 00:03:09,656
In that cave lived a gentle nymph named
Maia.

44
00:03:09,706 --> 00:03:16,019
She was shy. She liked her privacy. She
didn't go to the big loud parties on Mount

45
00:03:16,069 --> 00:03:22,459
Olympus. She mostly stayed home, in the
cool dark, listening to the trees.

46
00:03:22,509 --> 00:03:28,900
And one morning, just as the sky turned from
grey to pink to gold, Maia had a baby.

47
00:03:28,950 --> 00:03:32,656
A perfectly ordinary looking baby. Round
cheeks.

48
00:03:32,706 --> 00:03:38,943
Tiny fists. A little tuft of dark hair that
stuck straight up, as if he'd already

49
00:03:38,993 --> 00:03:41,397
had a very long night.

50
00:03:41,447 --> 00:03:45,614
His father was Zeus, king of the gods.
And his name...

51
00:03:45,664 --> 00:03:46,534
was Hermes.

52
00:03:46,984 --> 00:03:50,408
HYPNOS: Cute baby. Suspicious hair.

53
00:03:50,858 --> 00:03:56,733
Maia wrapped him in soft swaddling cloths,
the way you wrap babies, so they feel

54
00:03:56,783 --> 00:04:02,218
safe and snug. She laid him in a little
cradle woven from willow branches.

55
00:04:02,268 --> 00:04:08,143
She kissed his forehead. And then, because
she'd had a very big morning, she lay

56
00:04:08,193 --> 00:04:09,825
down to rest.

57
00:04:09,875 --> 00:04:13,482
Now... a normal baby, at this point,
would sleep.

58
00:04:13,932 --> 00:04:17,121
HYPNOS: Yes. Good. That's the correct
move.

59
00:04:17,571 --> 00:04:19,637
Hermes did not sleep.

60
00:04:20,087 --> 00:04:22,806
HYPNOS: Of course he didn't.

61
00:04:23,256 --> 00:04:27,481
He waited until his mother's breathing
was slow and even.

62
00:04:27,531 --> 00:04:30,798
Then he wiggled one arm free of the
blanket.

63
00:04:30,848 --> 00:04:36,473
Then the other arm. Then, very carefully,
he climbed up over the side of the

64
00:04:36,523 --> 00:04:42,738
cradle, flopped down onto the cave floor,
and toddled off toward the light.

65
00:04:42,788 --> 00:04:45,097
He was about... four hours old.

66
00:04:51,347 --> 00:04:56,584
HYPNOS: And here we observe the wild
newborn god, leaving the nest far too early.

67
00:04:56,634 --> 00:04:59,656
HYPNOS: A bold strategy. Let's see if it
pays off.

68
00:05:00,106 --> 00:05:06,041
At the mouth of the cave, sitting in the
morning sun, munching happily on the

69
00:05:06,091 --> 00:05:12,256
grass, was a tortoise. A big, slow,
peaceful tortoise, with a beautiful speckled

70
00:05:12,306 --> 00:05:13,484
shell.

71
00:05:13,534 --> 00:05:18,241
Hermes looked at the tortoise. The
tortoise looked at Hermes.

72
00:05:18,291 --> 00:05:23,305
And Hermes smiled the kind of smile that
makes grown-ups nervous.

73
00:05:23,755 --> 00:05:26,552
HYPNOS: Oh no. Not the tortoise.

74
00:05:27,002 --> 00:05:32,759
Now, the old stories are a little bit vague
about what happened next, and I think

75
00:05:32,809 --> 00:05:35,945
we'll leave them that way. Let's just
say...

76
00:05:35,995 --> 00:05:39,202
the tortoise retired from the shell
business.

77
00:05:39,252 --> 00:05:40,902
And Hermes had a shell.

78
00:05:41,352 --> 00:05:43,992
HYPNOS: We're going to respect the
tortoise's privacy.

79
00:05:44,442 --> 00:05:49,469
Hermes carried that shell back into the
cave, and he got to work.

80
00:05:49,519 --> 00:05:55,700
He cut two curved pieces of reed and fixed
them into the shell like little arms.

81
00:05:55,750 --> 00:06:01,854
He laid a crossbar across the top. He
stretched a smooth piece of hide over the

82
00:06:01,904 --> 00:06:06,700
hollow, so it would sing. And then he
tied on seven strings...

83
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:10,238
one... two... three... four... five...
six...

84
00:06:10,288 --> 00:06:11,469
seven.

85
00:06:11,519 --> 00:06:15,084
He plucked one. It hummed. He plucked
another.

86
00:06:15,134 --> 00:06:21,315
It shimmered. And then, sitting
cross-legged on the floor of the cave, this tiny

87
00:06:21,365 --> 00:06:23,853
baby god began to play.

88
00:06:23,903 --> 00:06:27,468
It was the very first lyre in the whole
world.

89
00:06:27,918 --> 00:06:32,818
HYPNOS: By lunchtime. He invented an
instrument by lunchtime.

90
00:06:32,868 --> 00:06:36,851
HYPNOS: I invented a new way to fold a
pillow once.

91
00:06:36,901 --> 00:06:38,317
HYPNOS: Took me a week.

92
00:06:38,767 --> 00:06:44,145
Hermes sang along, too. He made up a song
about his mother and his father...

93
00:06:44,195 --> 00:06:49,855
about the cave and its cool dark corners,
about the pots and the shelves and the

94
00:06:49,905 --> 00:06:54,014
shining cauldrons. A cozy little song
about home.

95
00:06:54,064 --> 00:06:58,597
But even while he was singing it... his
mind was somewhere else.

96
00:06:59,047 --> 00:07:02,471
HYPNOS: I know that look. That's a snack
look.

97
00:07:02,921 --> 00:07:05,838
That's exactly right. Hermes was hungry.

98
00:07:05,888 --> 00:07:11,484
And he wasn't thinking about milk. He was
thinking about something much, much

99
00:07:11,534 --> 00:07:16,912
bigger. He set the lyre down in his
cradle, and he crept back out into the

100
00:07:16,962 --> 00:07:17,712
afternoon.

101
00:07:24,358 --> 00:07:30,010
Now, far to the north, past the hills and
the rivers, there was a place called

102
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:34,629
Pieria. A land of long green meadows and
shady mountain slopes.

103
00:07:34,679 --> 00:07:39,536
And in those meadows grazed a very
special herd of cattle.

104
00:07:39,586 --> 00:07:43,650
These were not ordinary cows. They
belonged to the gods.

105
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:49,640
They had glossy coats and gentle eyes, and
they never got sick, and they never got

106
00:07:49,690 --> 00:07:52,671
old. And the one who looked after them...

107
00:07:52,721 --> 00:07:54,114
was the god Apollo.

108
00:07:54,564 --> 00:08:01,304
HYPNOS: Apollo. God of music, poetry,
light, prophecy, archery, medicine...

109
00:08:01,354 --> 00:08:03,500
HYPNOS: and apparently, cows.

110
00:08:03,950 --> 00:08:09,568
Apollo was Hermes's big half-brother. Tall,
golden, glowing a little bit all the

111
00:08:09,618 --> 00:08:13,696
time, the way some people glow after a
good night's sleep.

112
00:08:14,146 --> 00:08:17,952
HYPNOS: He glows because he never sleeps.
Different thing.

113
00:08:18,002 --> 00:08:19,086
HYPNOS: Very annoying.

114
00:08:19,536 --> 00:08:24,822
Just as the sun was setting, little
Hermes arrived at the edge of the meadow.

115
00:08:24,872 --> 00:08:30,501
He looked out over the herd... hundreds of
beautiful cows, munching quietly in the

116
00:08:30,551 --> 00:08:34,401
golden light. And from all of them, he
picked out fifty.

117
00:08:34,851 --> 00:08:35,716
HYPNOS: Fifty.

118
00:08:36,166 --> 00:08:37,108
Fifty.

119
00:08:37,558 --> 00:08:40,277
HYPNOS: He's the size of a loaf of bread.

120
00:08:40,727 --> 00:08:46,149
And here's the clever part. Hermes knew
that in the morning, Apollo would come

121
00:08:46,199 --> 00:08:49,543
looking. And Apollo would follow the
hoofprints.

122
00:08:49,593 --> 00:08:54,530
So Hermes turned the cows around... and
walked them backwards.

123
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:59,240
All fifty of them. Backwards. Through the
meadow and over the hill.

124
00:08:59,290 --> 00:09:04,851
So the hoofprints would look like the cows
had walked into the field, not out of

125
00:09:04,901 --> 00:09:05,651
it.

126
00:09:11,378 --> 00:09:15,106
HYPNOS: Fifty cows, moonwalking through
the night.

127
00:09:15,156 --> 00:09:18,094
HYPNOS: Very smooth. Very silent. Mostly.

128
00:09:18,544 --> 00:09:23,088
But that left one more problem. Hermes's
own little footprints.

129
00:09:23,138 --> 00:09:28,832
So he stopped by a thicket and gathered
branches of tamarisk and sweet-smelling

130
00:09:28,882 --> 00:09:34,862
myrtle, and he wove them together into the
biggest, strangest sandals you have ever

131
00:09:34,912 --> 00:09:39,528
seen. Great leafy bundles, strapped onto
his tiny feet.

132
00:09:39,578 --> 00:09:43,477
Now, instead of a baby's footprints, he
left behind...

133
00:09:43,527 --> 00:09:49,005
big, smudgy, mysterious marks. Like a
small walking bush had passed through.

134
00:09:49,455 --> 00:09:53,038
HYPNOS: A heist. Our baby is running a
full heist.

135
00:09:53,088 --> 00:09:54,812
HYPNOS: Cue the slow motion.

136
00:10:01,062 --> 00:10:05,260
All night long, Hermes drove the cows
across the land.

137
00:10:05,310 --> 00:10:08,736
Over shadowy hills. Through echoing
valleys.

138
00:10:08,786 --> 00:10:14,143
Across flowery plains. The moon came up
and watched them go.

139
00:10:14,193 --> 00:10:20,245
And near a place called Onchestus, they
passed an old man, working late in his

140
00:10:20,295 --> 00:10:24,261
vineyard, tying up his vines by moonlight.

141
00:10:24,311 --> 00:10:29,436
The old man stared. He saw a herd of cows
walking the wrong way...

142
00:10:29,486 --> 00:10:33,221
and behind them, a baby... in giant leafy
shoes.

143
00:10:33,671 --> 00:10:35,972
HYPNOS: That's a lot for one evening.

144
00:10:36,422 --> 00:10:42,242
Hermes stopped. He looked up at the old man
with big, innocent eyes, and he said,

145
00:10:42,292 --> 00:10:45,321
very politely: "Good evening, grandfather.

146
00:10:45,371 --> 00:10:50,618
Your vines look wonderful. You'll have so
many grapes this year.

147
00:10:50,668 --> 00:10:54,054
And if anybody asks... you didn't see
anything.

148
00:10:54,104 --> 00:10:57,061
And you didn't hear anything. All right?"

149
00:10:57,511 --> 00:11:01,980
HYPNOS: The baby threatened a farmer.
Politely. On his first day.

150
00:11:02,430 --> 00:11:07,788
And then he walked on, into the night,
with his fifty backwards cows.

151
00:11:14,438 --> 00:11:18,025
Just before dawn, they reached the river
Alpheus.

152
00:11:18,075 --> 00:11:24,135
A wide, calm river, with soft grassy banks
and tall rushes whispering at the edges.

153
00:11:24,185 --> 00:11:30,100
Hermes let the cows drink and graze, and
then he led them into a big, high-roofed

154
00:11:30,150 --> 00:11:34,683
cave by the water, and tucked them away
out of sight.

155
00:11:34,733 --> 00:11:37,884
By now, the baby god was very, very
hungry.

156
00:11:37,934 --> 00:11:43,631
So he gathered some dry wood. He took a
stick of laurel, and he rubbed it hard

157
00:11:43,681 --> 00:11:47,341
against another piece of wood... back and
forth...

158
00:11:47,391 --> 00:11:52,579
back and forth... until a curl of smoke
rose up, and then a tiny flame.

159
00:11:53,029 --> 00:11:56,944
HYPNOS: And just like that, he invented
fire sticks.

160
00:11:56,994 --> 00:11:59,323
HYPNOS: Before breakfast.

161
00:11:59,373 --> 00:12:04,786
HYPNOS: I need to lie down. I am lying
down. I need to lie down more.

162
00:12:05,236 --> 00:12:11,202
The old stories say that Hermes cooked a
feast there by the river, and he divided

163
00:12:11,252 --> 00:12:15,824
it into twelve equal portions, one for
each of the great gods.

164
00:12:15,874 --> 00:12:19,492
A little offering. Very neat, very fair.

165
00:12:19,542 --> 00:12:22,427
And then... he didn't eat a bite of it.

166
00:12:22,877 --> 00:12:25,909
HYPNOS: He did all that... and then he
didn't eat?

167
00:12:26,359 --> 00:12:29,343
He was a god. Gods don't really need to
eat.

168
00:12:29,393 --> 00:12:32,579
I think he just wanted to prove he could
do it.

169
00:12:33,029 --> 00:12:35,094
HYPNOS: Classic younger sibling.

170
00:12:35,544 --> 00:12:40,966
Then Hermes stamped out the fire,
scattered the ashes, and threw his big leafy

171
00:12:41,016 --> 00:12:45,537
sandals into the river, where they
floated away with the current.

172
00:12:45,587 --> 00:12:51,148
No evidence. And as the first light began
to glow over the hills, he ran all the

173
00:12:51,198 --> 00:12:53,641
way home to Mount Kyllene.

174
00:12:53,691 --> 00:12:57,104
When he reached the cave, he didn't use
the door.

175
00:12:57,154 --> 00:13:02,229
He made himself small and soft and quiet,
like a breath of autumn wind...

176
00:13:02,279 --> 00:13:07,286
like a curl of morning mist... and he
slipped right through the keyhole.

177
00:13:13,536 --> 00:13:17,174
HYPNOS: Through the keyhole. Nobody is
talking about the keyhole.

178
00:13:17,224 --> 00:13:20,800
HYPNOS: That's the most impressive thing
that's happened so far.

179
00:13:21,250 --> 00:13:25,989
He tiptoed across the floor. He climbed
back into his willow cradle.

180
00:13:26,039 --> 00:13:31,541
He wrapped the swaddling cloths around his
shoulders, tucked the lyre under his

181
00:13:31,591 --> 00:13:37,232
arm, and lay very still, playing with the
edge of his blanket, like the sweetest,

182
00:13:37,282 --> 00:13:40,425
most innocent baby in all of Greece.

183
00:13:40,475 --> 00:13:42,785
But mothers always know.

184
00:13:42,835 --> 00:13:48,407
Maia opened one eye. "And where," she said
quietly, "have you been all night, my

185
00:13:48,457 --> 00:13:49,726
little trickster?"

186
00:13:50,176 --> 00:13:51,275
HYPNOS: Busted.

187
00:13:51,725 --> 00:13:56,493
Hermes didn't even blink. "Mother," he
said, "don't worry about me.

188
00:13:56,543 --> 00:14:00,673
I've decided what I'm going to be. I'm
going to be clever.

189
00:14:00,723 --> 00:14:06,625
I'm going to be rich. And we are not going
to live in a dark little cave forever...

190
00:14:06,675 --> 00:14:12,577
we're going to live with the gods on
Olympus, with all the good blankets."

191
00:14:12,627 --> 00:14:16,615
Maia sighed the long, soft sigh that
every mother knows.

192
00:14:16,665 --> 00:14:18,032
And she let him be.

193
00:14:18,482 --> 00:14:24,310
HYPNOS: The good blankets. Okay. I
understand him a little better now.

194
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:34,403
Meanwhile, back in Pieria, the sun came
up.

195
00:14:34,453 --> 00:14:38,453
And Apollo went out to check on his cows.

196
00:14:38,503 --> 00:14:42,026
He counted them once. He counted them
twice.

197
00:14:42,076 --> 00:14:45,440
And his golden face went very, very still.

198
00:14:45,490 --> 00:14:47,028
Fifty were missing.

199
00:14:47,478 --> 00:14:49,387
HYPNOS: Cue the detective music.

200
00:14:55,637 --> 00:14:59,536
Apollo searched the meadow. He found the
hoofprints...

201
00:14:59,586 --> 00:15:05,209
but they were all pointing the wrong way,
as if fifty cows had walked into the

202
00:15:05,259 --> 00:15:08,512
field and then simply vanished into thin
air.

203
00:15:08,562 --> 00:15:13,322
And next to them were those strange,
smudgy, leafy marks.

204
00:15:13,372 --> 00:15:18,995
Apollo crouched down and frowned. "These
aren't the tracks of a man," he said.

205
00:15:19,045 --> 00:15:22,298
"Or a woman. Or a wolf, or a bear, or a
lion.

206
00:15:22,348 --> 00:15:24,739
What on earth walks like a bush?"

207
00:15:25,189 --> 00:15:29,502
HYPNOS: A toddler. In shoes he made
himself. Next question.

208
00:15:29,952 --> 00:15:35,610
So Apollo followed the trail, and he came
to the old man in the vineyard at

209
00:15:35,660 --> 00:15:39,590
Onchestus. "Good morning, grandfather,"
said Apollo.

210
00:15:39,640 --> 00:15:44,922
"Did you see anyone pass by last night,
with a herd of cows?"

211
00:15:44,972 --> 00:15:51,006
The old man scratched his beard. "Well," he
said slowly, "I see a lot of things.

212
00:15:51,056 --> 00:15:55,587
My eyes aren't what they were. But... I
think I saw a child.

213
00:15:55,637 --> 00:15:59,642
A very small child. Walking zigzag all
over the road.

214
00:15:59,692 --> 00:16:02,346
And the cows were going backwards."

215
00:16:02,796 --> 00:16:08,545
HYPNOS: So much for "you didn't see
anything." Grandpa folded in one question.

216
00:16:08,995 --> 00:16:13,667
And right then, a bird with long wings
flew across the sky...

217
00:16:13,717 --> 00:16:19,379
and Apollo, who could read signs and
omens, understood everything at once.

218
00:16:19,429 --> 00:16:22,578
The thief was a newborn. And the thief...

219
00:16:22,628 --> 00:16:24,710
was a son of Zeus.

220
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:30,574
Apollo wrapped himself in a purple cloud
and flew straight to Mount Kyllene.

221
00:16:31,224 --> 00:16:34,201
Inside the cave, Hermes heard him coming.

222
00:16:34,251 --> 00:16:38,596
He curled himself up into a tiny ball
inside his blankets...

223
00:16:38,646 --> 00:16:41,695
the way warm embers hide under grey ash...

224
00:16:41,745 --> 00:16:46,090
and he squeezed his eyes shut. Pretending
to be fast asleep.

225
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:51,639
HYPNOS: Oh, now this is my area. Let's
see the technique.

226
00:16:51,689 --> 00:16:55,217
HYPNOS: Eyes closed, good. Breathing
slow, nice.

227
00:16:55,267 --> 00:16:59,319
HYPNOS: Tiny snore? Too much. Never do
the tiny snore.

228
00:16:59,369 --> 00:17:02,373
HYPNOS: Amateurs always do the tiny snore.

229
00:17:08,623 --> 00:17:12,712
Apollo strode into the cave. He searched
every corner.

230
00:17:12,762 --> 00:17:19,033
He opened every cupboard. He found jars of
sweet nectar and ambrosia, and chests of

231
00:17:19,083 --> 00:17:23,172
gold and silver, and the shimmering
dresses of Maia...

232
00:17:23,222 --> 00:17:25,504
but not a single cow.

233
00:17:25,554 --> 00:17:31,148
Then he turned to the cradle. "Little
one," he said, "you in the blankets.

234
00:17:31,198 --> 00:17:37,469
Tell me where my cows are. Right now. Or you
and I are going to have a very serious

235
00:17:37,519 --> 00:17:38,269
problem."

236
00:17:38,672 --> 00:17:40,581
HYPNOS: Big brother energy.

237
00:17:41,031 --> 00:17:46,750
And Hermes, the baby who was only one day
old, sat up in his cradle, blinked his

238
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:49,456
big innocent eyes, and said:

239
00:17:49,506 --> 00:17:52,447
"Son of Leto, what harsh words are these?

240
00:17:52,497 --> 00:17:55,438
Cows? What cows? I haven't seen any cows.

241
00:17:55,488 --> 00:18:01,136
I haven't heard about any cows. I
wouldn't know a cow if it sat on me.

242
00:18:01,186 --> 00:18:04,198
Look at me. Do I look like a cattle thief?

243
00:18:04,248 --> 00:18:10,608
I'm a baby. I was born yesterday. My feet
are soft, and the ground is very hard.

244
00:18:10,658 --> 00:18:15,379
I don't care about cows. The only things
I care about are sleep...

245
00:18:15,429 --> 00:18:20,009
and my mother's milk... and warm blankets
around my shoulders...

246
00:18:20,059 --> 00:18:21,575
and nice warm baths."

247
00:18:22,025 --> 00:18:26,623
HYPNOS: Okay. I'm not going to lie.
That's a beautiful list.

248
00:18:26,673 --> 00:18:32,936
HYPNOS: Sleep, milk, blankets, warm
baths. That's my whole philosophy.

249
00:18:32,986 --> 00:18:36,970
HYPNOS: He's still guilty. But it's a
beautiful list.

250
00:18:37,420 --> 00:18:41,196
Apollo actually laughed. He couldn't help
it.

251
00:18:41,246 --> 00:18:47,518
He reached down and scooped the baby
right up out of the cradle...

252
00:18:47,568 --> 00:18:52,010
and at that exact moment, Hermes made a
very small...

253
00:18:52,060 --> 00:18:55,420
very rude... noise. And then he sneezed.

254
00:18:55,870 --> 00:18:57,701
HYPNOS: Classic.

255
00:18:58,151 --> 00:19:03,445
Apollo was so surprised that he put the
baby down on the ground, rather quickly.

256
00:19:03,495 --> 00:19:07,271
And Hermes, quick as anything, jumped up
and said: "Fine.

257
00:19:07,321 --> 00:19:11,032
If you don't believe me... let's go and
ask our father."

258
00:19:17,682 --> 00:19:21,094
So the two brothers went up to Mount
Olympus.

259
00:19:21,144 --> 00:19:27,191
Up through the clouds, up to the
sweet-smelling heights, where the gods lived in

260
00:19:27,241 --> 00:19:34,041
golden halls. And there, on his great
shining throne, sat Zeus, king of the gods.

261
00:19:34,091 --> 00:19:37,428
Apollo spoke first. He told the whole
story.

262
00:19:37,478 --> 00:19:40,740
The missing cows. The backwards
hoofprints.

263
00:19:40,790 --> 00:19:44,956
The leafy footprints. The old man. The
very rude noise.

264
00:19:45,406 --> 00:19:49,222
HYPNOS: He included the noise. In the
official testimony.

265
00:19:49,672 --> 00:19:55,441
And then it was Hermes's turn. He stood
up very straight, all of one day old,

266
00:19:55,491 --> 00:20:01,260
wrapped in his little blanket, and he
said: "Father Zeus, I will tell you the

267
00:20:01,310 --> 00:20:05,661
truth. I am a very honest baby. I could
never tell a lie."

268
00:20:11,911 --> 00:20:13,977
HYPNOS: Objection. Lying.

269
00:20:14,427 --> 00:20:20,323
"This big strong god came into our home,"
said Hermes, "and frightened my poor

270
00:20:20,373 --> 00:20:24,086
mother, and threatened me... a tiny
newborn baby.

271
00:20:24,136 --> 00:20:27,925
I did not take his cows home. I swear it.

272
00:20:27,975 --> 00:20:31,387
And you know, Father, I love him very
much...

273
00:20:31,437 --> 00:20:34,021
but he's being a little bit mean."

274
00:20:34,471 --> 00:20:38,913
HYPNOS: Look at that. Technically true.
He didn't take them home.

275
00:20:38,963 --> 00:20:43,172
HYPNOS: He took them to a different cave.
Lawyers, take notes.

276
00:20:43,622 --> 00:20:49,355
And Zeus, the great king of all the gods,
the thunderer, the cloud-gatherer...

277
00:20:49,405 --> 00:20:55,430
looked down at his tiny son, standing there
in his blanket, telling this enormous,

278
00:20:55,480 --> 00:20:59,602
clever, ridiculous fib with a perfectly
straight face...

279
00:20:59,652 --> 00:21:05,751
and Zeus laughed. He laughed so hard that
the clouds around Olympus shook.

280
00:21:05,801 --> 00:21:11,387
Then he wiped his eyes, and he said,
kindly but firmly: "All right, you two.

281
00:21:11,437 --> 00:21:15,999
Enough. Hermes... go and show your
brother where the cows are.

282
00:21:16,049 --> 00:21:18,341
And then make up. Both of you."

283
00:21:18,791 --> 00:21:22,712
HYPNOS: And Hermes did. Because nobody
argues with Dad.

284
00:21:29,362 --> 00:21:34,934
So the two brothers walked together, all the
way back down to the river Alpheus, in

285
00:21:34,984 --> 00:21:40,288
the warm afternoon light. Hermes led
Apollo to the cave by the water, and there

286
00:21:40,338 --> 00:21:45,709
were the cows, safe and sound, munching on
the sweet grass and the river rushes,

287
00:21:45,759 --> 00:21:49,524
looking very pleased with their little
holiday.

288
00:21:49,574 --> 00:21:55,146
Apollo was still a little bit annoyed. He
picked some long willow branches, meaning

289
00:21:55,196 --> 00:22:00,166
to tie up the little thief. But the old
stories say that something magical

290
00:22:00,216 --> 00:22:05,587
happened. The willow bands slipped off, and
fell to the ground, and took root...

291
00:22:05,637 --> 00:22:10,874
and grew. Green and leafy and soft, all
around the resting cows, like a living

292
00:22:10,924 --> 00:22:11,674
fence.

293
00:22:12,393 --> 00:22:16,945
HYPNOS: Even the plants are on his side.

294
00:22:16,995 --> 00:22:20,232
HYPNOS: Unbelievable. Sorry. Yawning.

295
00:22:21,282 --> 00:22:25,880
Then Hermes did something very wise. He
didn't argue anymore.

296
00:22:25,930 --> 00:22:32,102
He didn't make any more excuses. He just sat
down on a rock by the river, took out

297
00:22:32,152 --> 00:22:36,825
his tortoiseshell lyre... and began to
play.

298
00:22:36,875 --> 00:22:39,374
And oh... it was lovely.

299
00:22:39,424 --> 00:22:44,397
The notes rippled out across the water
like little rings of light.

300
00:22:44,447 --> 00:22:48,145
Soft and sweet and warm. And Hermes sang
along...

301
00:22:48,195 --> 00:22:54,368
a gentle song about the beginning of the
world, about the earth and the sky, about

302
00:22:54,418 --> 00:23:01,265
the gods, each one in turn, and the long,
slow story of how everything came to be.

303
00:23:01,315 --> 00:23:06,362
Apollo, the god of music himself, had
never heard anything like it.

304
00:23:06,412 --> 00:23:11,010
All the crossness drained out of him. His
shoulders softened.

305
00:23:11,060 --> 00:23:14,234
He sat down on the grass. And he listened.

306
00:23:15,284 --> 00:23:19,597
HYPNOS: Yeah. That's... that's actually
nice. I'll allow it.

307
00:23:20,647 --> 00:23:24,698
When the song ended, Apollo said quietly,
"Little brother...

308
00:23:24,748 --> 00:23:27,858
that instrument is worth fifty cows."

309
00:23:27,908 --> 00:23:30,682
And Hermes smiled, and held out the lyre.

310
00:23:30,732 --> 00:23:33,842
"Then it's yours," he said. "Keep it.
Play it.

311
00:23:33,892 --> 00:23:36,801
Teach the whole world to love it."

312
00:23:36,851 --> 00:23:42,449
Apollo took the lyre in his golden hands,
and he played a few notes of his own, and

313
00:23:42,499 --> 00:23:47,828
he smiled the biggest, warmest smile. And
in return, he gave Hermes the shining

314
00:23:47,878 --> 00:23:52,534
whip of the herdsman, and made him the
keeper of the cattle.

315
00:23:52,584 --> 00:23:57,645
So the cows that Hermes had stolen...
were now Hermes's cows to look after.

316
00:23:57,695 --> 00:23:58,788
Fair and square.

317
00:23:59,838 --> 00:24:02,158
HYPNOS: Everybody wins.

318
00:24:02,208 --> 00:24:04,150
HYPNOS: Except the tortoise.

319
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:07,216
Except the tortoise.

320
00:24:07,266 --> 00:24:11,003
And Hermes, being Hermes, couldn't sit
still for long.

321
00:24:11,053 --> 00:24:16,580
While his cows grazed by the river, he cut
some reeds of different lengths, tied

322
00:24:16,630 --> 00:24:20,298
them side by side, and blew softly across
the tops...

323
00:24:20,348 --> 00:24:25,187
and out came a low, breathy, whispery
music, like wind over the hills.

324
00:24:25,237 --> 00:24:28,286
He'd invented the shepherd's pipes.

325
00:24:28,336 --> 00:24:31,797
Apollo listened, and laughed, and loved
those too.

326
00:24:31,847 --> 00:24:37,306
And so he gave his little brother a
beautiful golden staff, and the two of them

327
00:24:37,356 --> 00:24:42,057
made a promise... no more stealing, no
more tricks between brothers.

328
00:24:42,107 --> 00:24:45,431
From now on, they would look out for each
other.

329
00:24:46,481 --> 00:24:51,745
HYPNOS: I like that ending. Brothers.
Music. Cows.

330
00:24:51,795 --> 00:24:54,712
HYPNOS: Very... very wholesome.

331
00:24:55,762 --> 00:24:59,467
And from that day on, Hermes had a job on
Mount Olympus.

332
00:24:59,517 --> 00:25:04,804
He became the messenger of the gods, quick
as thought, with wings on his sandals

333
00:25:04,854 --> 00:25:10,272
and wings on his hat. He became the friend
of travelers, and shepherds, and anyone

334
00:25:10,322 --> 00:25:12,842
walking a long road at night.

335
00:25:12,892 --> 00:25:16,334
And the old songs say something else
about him, too.

336
00:25:16,384 --> 00:25:21,209
They call him a watcher in the night...
and a bringer of dreams.

337
00:25:21,259 --> 00:25:26,085
With his golden staff, he could touch the
eyes of anyone who was tired...

338
00:25:26,135 --> 00:25:28,391
and send them gently off to sleep.

339
00:25:29,441 --> 00:25:32,405
HYPNOS: Hey... that's my job.

340
00:25:32,455 --> 00:25:38,142
HYPNOS: That's... okay. He can help.
I'm... a little tired anyway.

341
00:25:45,192 --> 00:25:51,342
And so the evening came again, at the end
of that very long first day.

342
00:25:51,392 --> 00:25:56,225
The sun sank down behind the hills, and
the sky turned gold...

343
00:25:56,275 --> 00:26:00,410
and then rose... and then a deep, soft
blue.

344
00:26:00,460 --> 00:26:03,898
One by one, the stars came out over
Arcadia.

345
00:26:05,548 --> 00:26:07,300
HYPNOS: ...goodnight, everybody.

346
00:26:08,950 --> 00:26:15,225
Down by the river Alpheus, the cows lay down
in the cool grass, folding their legs

347
00:26:15,275 --> 00:26:19,721
beneath them, one by one... fifty soft
shapes in the dusk.

348
00:26:19,771 --> 00:26:23,303
Their breathing slowed. Their eyes grew
heavy.

349
00:26:23,353 --> 00:26:29,780
The willow fence rustled gently around
them in the evening breeze.

350
00:26:29,830 --> 00:26:35,724
Far away in Pieria, Apollo sat on a quiet
hill with his new lyre on his knee.

351
00:26:35,774 --> 00:26:39,306
He played one soft note... and then
another...

352
00:26:39,356 --> 00:26:45,250
slow and low and peaceful... letting each
note hang in the cool air before he

353
00:26:45,300 --> 00:26:47,917
played the next.

354
00:26:47,967 --> 00:26:52,794
Up on Mount Olympus, the gods were
settling down for the night.

355
00:26:52,844 --> 00:26:59,195
The golden halls grew dim. The clouds
drifted slowly past the windows like big soft

356
00:26:59,245 --> 00:27:04,606
pillows. And somewhere, Zeus was still
chuckling quietly to himself...

357
00:27:04,656 --> 00:27:07,806
about a baby... and fifty backwards cows.

358
00:27:09,456 --> 00:27:15,402
And in the cave on Mount Kyllene, behind
the curtain of ivy, Maia lit a single

359
00:27:15,452 --> 00:27:18,969
small lamp. She looked into the willow
cradle.

360
00:27:19,019 --> 00:27:21,321
And there was Hermes.

361
00:27:21,371 --> 00:27:25,647
His tuft of hair was sticking up, the way
it always did.

362
00:27:25,697 --> 00:27:29,366
His tiny fists were curled up beside his
cheeks.

363
00:27:29,416 --> 00:27:33,085
And for the very first time since he was
born...

364
00:27:33,135 --> 00:27:35,968
he was fast asleep.

365
00:27:36,018 --> 00:27:39,232
Not pretending. Not peeking. Not planning.

366
00:27:39,282 --> 00:27:41,281
Just... sleeping.

367
00:27:41,331 --> 00:27:45,683
Breathing slow... and deep... and even.

368
00:27:45,733 --> 00:27:49,401
Maia tucked the blanket up around his
shoulders.

369
00:27:49,451 --> 00:27:54,941
She kissed the top of his head. And she
whispered, "Goodnight, my little

370
00:27:54,991 --> 00:27:55,852
trickster."

371
00:27:57,502 --> 00:28:04,028
And far, far away, in a quiet cave at the
edge of the world, beside a slow silver

372
00:28:04,078 --> 00:28:07,557
river, where the poppies nod in the
dark...

373
00:28:07,607 --> 00:28:11,487
the god of sleep had fallen asleep too.

374
00:28:11,537 --> 00:28:15,817
Wings folded. Blanket pulled up to his
chin.

375
00:28:15,867 --> 00:28:18,063
Snoring... just a tiny bit.

376
00:28:19,713 --> 00:28:22,086
You can let go now, too.

377
00:28:22,136 --> 00:28:25,935
There's nothing left to do tonight. No
cows to count.

378
00:28:25,985 --> 00:28:29,856
No tracks to follow. No one to fool.

379
00:28:29,906 --> 00:28:32,992
Just the soft dark... and the slow
river...

380
00:28:33,042 --> 00:28:36,912
and the warm weight of your blanket.

381
00:28:36,962 --> 00:28:41,831
Let your breathing slow down... like the
cows by the river.

382
00:28:41,881 --> 00:28:43,470
Breathe in...

383
00:28:43,520 --> 00:28:45,395
and out.

384
00:28:45,445 --> 00:28:46,464
In...

385
00:28:46,514 --> 00:28:48,388
and out.

386
00:28:48,438 --> 00:28:52,024
Let your hands get heavy. Let your feet
get heavy.

387
00:28:52,074 --> 00:28:57,940
Let your thoughts float away like leafy
sandals drifting down the river Alpheus...

388
00:28:57,990 --> 00:29:02,359
further... and further... and further
away.

389
00:29:02,409 --> 00:29:08,703
The stars are out. The lamp is low.
Everyone in the story is sleeping.

390
00:29:08,753 --> 00:29:11,911
And now... it's your turn.

391
00:29:11,961 --> 00:29:13,978
Goodnight.

392
00:29:14,028 --> 00:29:14,833
Sleep well.
